<p>“If God wills it and Sir Étienne judges me worthy, I may be knighted.”</p>
<p>Alaric frowned.</p>
<p>“What if he doesn’t?”</p>
<p>The boy looked genuinely confused by the question.</p>
<p>“Then I am not ready.”</p>
<p>Alaric thought of his own road to knighthood: his years as a craftsman, his training, the Order’s wars, his bond with Vaelor, and the moment when Gawain had finally raised him into the Order.</p>
<p>Different road, he thought. Perhaps not an easier one.</p>
<p>That evening King Charles invited the Dragon Riders to witness a knighting vigil at Notre-Dame du Lys.</p>
<p>The cathedral was even larger inside than it appeared from Bellacourt’s streets. Rows of pillars rose into shadow. Candles burned before painted saints and ancient kings. The Sacred Lily appeared everywhere: carved into stone, embroidered into banners and worked into colored glass.</p>
<p>The young man to be knighted entered without armor.</p>
<p>His sword was placed before the altar.</p>
<p>For most of the night he remained alone in prayer.</p>
<p>Alaric expected boredom. Instead he found himself impressed.</p>
<p>There were no cheering crowds, no tournament trumpets, no nobles displaying jewels. The vigil was deliberately quiet. The candidate was meant to consider what the sword was for, whom he would serve, and what kind of man he intended to become once people were required to call him knight.</p>
<p>Archbishop Anselm presided from a distance.</p>
<p>When he noticed Alaric watching, he approached.</p>
<p>“You seem surprised.”</p>
<p>“I thought Bretonnian knighthood was mostly banners and expensive horses.”</p>
<p>Anselm gave him a dry look.</p>
<p>“And I thought Dragon Riders were men who sat on monsters and burned their enemies from the sky.”</p>
<p>Alaric looked at him.</p>
<p>The Archbishop almost smiled.</p>
<p>“Perhaps,” Anselm said, “we are both learning.”</p>
<p>The next morning the cathedral filled.</p>
<p>The squire from the vigil knelt before his lord. A priest blessed the sword. Oaths were spoken: to defend the weak, uphold justice, remain faithful, honor rightful authority and keep one’s word.</p>
<p>Some of those promises sounded very familiar to Alaric.</p>
<p>Then came the accolade, and the young man rose as a knight.</p>
<p>Outside the cathedral, Bellacourt erupted into celebration.</p>
<p>Alaric walked beside Charles through the square.</p>
<p>“You approve?” the king asked.</p>
<p>“Of the oath, yes.”</p>
<p>“And the rest?”</p>
<p>Alaric glanced at the noble families surrounding the newly made knight.</p>
<p>“I’m still deciding.”</p>
<p>Charles nodded, apparently pleased by the answer.</p>
<p>Alaric had begun to understand the appeal of Bretonnian chivalry. It gave people a language for courage, duty and service. A child could watch a knight take his oath and understand that strength was supposed to mean responsibility.</p>
<p>But he could also see the danger.</p>
<p>Some boys had opportunities because their fathers possessed land and names. Some squires had been raised inside castles from childhood, while other children in Bellacourt would never come close to a sword except to repair one or carry it for someone richer.</p>
<p>The ideals were admirable.</p>
<p>Access to them was not equal.</p>
<p>That distinction stayed with Alaric as the Grand Tourney drew near.</p>
<p>He had expected to come to Bretonnia and show its knights what the Dragon Rider Order could do.</p>
<p>Instead, Bretonnia was already forcing him to reconsider what a knight was.</p>
<h1>Chapter 4 – Their First Jousting</h1>
<p>The Dragon Riders discovered their problem on the first morning of practice.</p>
<p>They did not know how to joust.</p>
<p>This surprised everyone, including them.</p>
<p>The Royal Fields outside Bellacourt had been transformed into a city of timber stands, colorful pavilions and long fenced lanes. Banners snapped above the field. Armorers worked beneath awnings. Stable boys hurried between rows of horses. Trumpeters practiced signals until even the horses seemed tired of hearing them.</p>
<p>Alaric stood beside a rack of tournament lances and lifted one.</p>
<p>It was longer than he expected.</p>
<p>“Too heavy?” asked a voice behind him.</p>
<p>Alaric turned.</p>
<p>The speaker was a broad-shouldered Bretonnian knight with a grey beard and the patient expression of a man accustomed to watching younger warriors make mistakes.</p>
<p>“Not heavy,” Alaric said.</p>
<p>“Good. Then you will have no excuse when you drop it.”</p>
<p>This was Master Bertrand, appointed by Charles to prepare the visiting Dragon Riders for the tourney.</p>
<p>Alaric liked him immediately.</p>
<p>The first exercise seemed simple. Mount the horse, lower the lance and strike a painted shield fixed to a post.</p>
<p>Alaric missed.</p>
<p>The lance passed neatly to the left.</p>
<p>Bertrand said nothing.</p>
<p>Alaric circled back and tried again.</p>
<p>This time he hit the edge and nearly twisted himself out of the saddle.</p>
<p>Bertrand nodded.</p>
<p>“Excellent.”</p>
<p>Alaric stared at him.</p>
<p>“I almost fell off.”</p>
<p>“Yes. Now you have begun to learn.”</p>
<p>The Dragon Riders were experienced warriors, but most of their mounted instincts came from dragons.</p>
<p>That turned out to be a disadvantage.</p>
<p>A dragon adjusted in the air beneath its rider. It banked, climbed and understood commands with an intelligence no horse possessed. A rider could shift his whole body during a turn and trust his dragon to answer.</p>
<p>A tournament horse required different balance. Different timing. Different restraint.</p>
<p>The lance made everything worse.</p>
<p>“You are all trying to ride as if the animal can read your mind,” Bertrand told them after an especially poor round.</p>
<p>“Ours usually can,” Alaric said.</p>
<p>“That is deeply unfair.”</p>
<p>By the third day, they were improving.</p>
<p>Bertrand taught them to lock their legs, carry the lance without fighting its weight, keep their eyes on the target and trust the horse’s line. They practiced against rings, posts and padded quintains before being allowed to charge another rider.</p>
<p>The first time Alaric faced a real opponent, he felt absurdly nervous.</p>
<p>He had fought demons.</p>
<p>He had crossed blades with monsters beneath mountains.</p>
<p>Yet now a cheerful knight from some duchy he had never heard of was galloping toward him with a wooden lance, and Alaric’s main concern was remembering not to lean as if Vaelor were about to bank left.</p>
<p>The impact exploded across his shield.</p>
<p>His own lance struck the other knight’s shoulder and shattered.</p>
<p>The crowd watching practice cheered.</p>
<p>Alaric remained in the saddle.</p>
<p>Bertrand raised both hands.</p>
<p>“A miracle.”</p>
<p>Alaric laughed despite himself.</p>
<p>The next lesson was heraldry.</p>
<p>This proved almost as complicated as jousting.</p>
<p>The Dragon Riders had their own symbols and colors, but the Bretonnian heralds insisted that tournament shields needed to be readable from a distance and distinct enough to identify each competitor immediately.</p>
<p>A royal herald laid sheets of painted designs across a table.</p>
<p>“Your personal arms,” he told Alaric, “should say who you are.”</p>
<p>“That sounds dangerous.”</p>
<p>“It is the entire purpose.”</p>
<p>Alaric looked through designs of beasts, stars, towers, swords, suns, rivers and trees.</p>
<p>In Bretonnia, heraldry was not decoration. It was identity made visible.</p>
<p>A knight’s shield could show family, marriage, allegiance, inheritance, victories and claims. Royal lilies were everywhere not simply as decoration, but because every repetition reminded people of the crown and the kingdom.</p>
<p>The herald offered Alaric several possible designs.</p>
<p>Alaric studied them for a long time, then pushed them back.</p>
<p>“Not yet,” he said.</p>
<p>The herald blinked. “You must carry something in the lists.”</p>
<p>“Then give me a plain tournament shield bearing the mark of the Dragon Rider Order. My own arms should mean something before I choose them.”</p>
<p>The herald considered that, then nodded.</p>
<p>It was another lesson Alaric would carry home: heraldry could be inherited, granted, claimed, or deliberately created. A symbol mattered because people agreed on what it meant.</p>
<p>By the eve of the tourney, the riders could pass as competent jousters.</p>
<p>Competent, Alaric discovered, was very different from excellent.</p>
<p>From the stands he watched Bretonnian champions train.</p>
<p>They had practiced this since youth. They knew exactly how much of the horse’s speed could be turned into the lance. They struck shields at precise angles. They recovered from impacts that would have thrown Alaric sideways three days earlier.</p>
<p>Their skill impressed him.</p>
<p>There was something healthy in discovering that a famous warrior could travel far enough from home to become a beginner again.</p>
<p>Master Bertrand joined him at the rail.</p>
<p>“You expected to win easily.”</p>
<p>“I expected not to embarrass myself.”</p>
<p>“That is a more achievable ambition.”</p>
<p>Alaric smiled.</p>
<p>Across the Royal Fields, the towers of Bellacourt rose behind the stands. Beyond them stood Côte de Lys and the spires of Notre-Dame du Lys.</p>
<p>For the moment, the city seemed peaceful.</p>
<p>The Church remained uncertain about the dragons. Charles continued receiving nobles and envoys from across the Bretonnian Lands. Every feast and ceremony carried meanings Alaric only partly understood.</p>
<p>But tomorrow, at least, the rules would be simple.</p>
<p>Two knights would ride toward one another.</p>
<p>One lance would meet one shield.</p>
<p>And Alaric, Dragon Rider Knight of the Valley, would try very hard not to fall off a horse.</p>
<h1>Chapter 5 – The Capital Markets</h1>
<p>The morning after the first rounds of the Grand Tourney, Alaric woke with an ache in his shoulder and a new respect for Bretonnian lances.</p>
<p>He had remained in the saddle.</p>
<p>That, Master Bertrand assured him, was already a respectable achievement.</p>
<p>“You struck your opponent cleanly,” Bertrand said over breakfast. “You merely struck him as if you were trying to move a mountain.”</p>
<p>“I usually fight things that need to be moved.”</p>
<p>“That explains a great deal.”</p>
<p>There were no jousts scheduled for Alaric until the following day, so Gaston offered to show him another part of Bellacourt.</p>
<p>“The Royal Fields show you what Bretonnia thinks a knight should look like,” the herald said. “The markets show you what keeps him alive.”</p>
<p>Bellacourt’s markets seemed large enough to feed a kingdom.</p>
<p>Farmers entered through the gates before sunrise with carts of grain, onions, apples, cheeses, chickens and barrels of salted meat. Fishmongers shouted beside bakers. Cloth merchants displayed wool, linen and imported silks. Smiths sold tools beside craftsmen whose work Alaric could not even identify.</p>
<p>The streets were organized by trade. One lane smelled of leather. Another rang with hammers. A third was filled with dyers whose vats turned water red, yellow and deep blue.</p>
<p>Alaric stopped beneath a wooden sign painted with a boot.</p>
<p>“Why are all the shoemakers here?”</p>
<p>“Because this is the shoemakers’ quarter.”</p>
<p>“I can see that.”</p>
<p>“They belong to a guild.”</p>
<p>Gaston explained that the guild decided who could open a workshop, who could call himself a master, how apprentices were trained, and what standards work had to meet.</p>
<p>“So they control their own trade?”</p>
<p>“In part.”</p>
<p>“And if I decide tomorrow that I want to make shoes?”</p>
<p>“You would first spend several years as an apprentice.”</p>
<p>Alaric looked offended.</p>
<p>“I know how leather works.”</p>
<p>“Then you will be an unusually promising apprentice.”</p>
<p>The system amused him until he understood how much of Bellacourt depended on it.</p>
<p>Guilds maintained workshops, trained craftsmen, supported widows of members, contributed money to city defenses and sometimes funded chapels, fountains or hospitals. Their banners appeared in civic processions beside those of nobles and clergy.</p>
<p>Yet they also protected their privileges fiercely.</p>
<p>A young cooper told Alaric that his father had worked barrels for twenty years but would probably never become a master because there were already enough masters in his guild.</p>
<p>“Enough according to whom?” Alaric asked.</p>
<p>The cooper shrugged.</p>
<p>“The masters.”</p>
<p>Bretonnia seemed full of arrangements like that.</p>
<p>Everywhere Alaric found useful institutions wrapped around old advantages.</p>
<p>Knights were expected to defend roads and villages, but they also paid fewer tolls. Noble families were expected to provide soldiers and govern estates, but inherited privileges that ordinary people could never earn. Guilds taught valuable skills, but could close doors behind themselves. The Church ran schools, hospitals, hostels and poor kitchens, yet owned lands and commanded obedience across borders.</p>
<p>Nothing was simple enough to be either good or bad.</p>
<p>At midday they reached the grain market.</p>
<p>Here the scale of the city became obvious.</p>
<p>Sacks were stacked beneath long covered halls. Clerks measured grain. Merchants argued over prices. Wagons waited for inspection before entering warehouses.</p>
<p>Near one gate, a monk and two lay brothers were loading sacks onto a cart marked with the Sacred Lily.</p>
<p>“Where is that going?” Alaric asked.</p>
<p>“To a hospital house in the southern quarter,” said Gaston. “The Church maintains several.”</p>
<p>A little farther on, another Church cart was being filled with dried beans, onions and bread.</p>
<p>Alaric watched it go.</p>
<p>He had spent days hearing Archbishop Anselm speak cautiously about dragons, doctrine and things the Church could not yet understand.</p>
<p>It was easy, from Côte de Lys, to imagine the Church as a collection of old men debating old books.</p>
<p>It looked different here.</p>
<p>A bell sounded from a nearby chapel.</p>
<p>A woman carrying two baskets stopped, made the sign of blessing and continued walking.</p>
<p>A merchant did the same.</p>
<p>So did a soldier.</p>
<p>None of them seemed to think much about it.</p>
<p>The Church was simply there, woven through the city in a thousand small ways.</p>
<p>That afternoon Alaric met King Charles near the merchants’ hall.</p>
<p>The king was inspecting preparations for a public feast connected with the tournament. He moved with only a small escort, though everyone recognized him.</p>
<p>“You have escaped the lists,” Charles said.</p>
<p>“For one day.”</p>
<p>“And what do you think of my capital?”</p>
<p>“It is complicated.”</p>
<p>Charles smiled. “Then you are beginning to understand it.”</p>
<p>They walked together for a time.</p>
<p>Alaric watched a group of pages carrying baskets for an elderly canon. Behind them, two richly dressed merchants argued with a tax clerk. Across the square, a nobleman’s servants cleared a path for his horse.</p>
<p>“In the Valley,” Alaric said, “we argue about who should do something. Here you first argue about who has the right to do it.”</p>
<p>Charles laughed.</p>
<p>“That may be the most accurate criticism of Bretonnia I have heard this year.”</p>
<p>“But people seem to know where they belong.”</p>
<p>“That is the strength of it.”</p>
<p>“And the weakness.”</p>
<p>“Yes.”</p>
<p>Charles did not sound offended.</p>
<p>That surprised Alaric.</p>
<p>They stopped near a fountain where children were filling small buckets.</p>
<p>Beyond them rose the towers of Notre-Dame du Lys.</p>
<p>“The old institutions make us slow,” Charles said. “They also mean that when a road fails, someone knows who must repair it. When a harvest fails, someone knows which granary is responsible. When a lord dies, someone knows who owes protection to the village beneath his walls. The problem begins when people remember the privilege and forget the duty.”</p>
<p>Alaric thought of the vigil in the cathedral.</p>
<p>Strength was supposed to mean responsibility.</p>
<p>Perhaps Bretonnia had built an entire civilization around that idea.</p>
<p>Perhaps it had also spent centuries finding ways to escape it.</p>
<p>Before he could answer, bells began ringing across the city.</p>
<p>Not the cheerful bells that had marked tournament victories.</p>
<p>These were slower.</p>
<p>More deliberate.</p>
<p>Gaston turned toward Notre-Dame du Lys.</p>
<p>Charles’s expression changed.</p>
<p>“What is it?” Alaric asked.</p>
<p>A royal messenger pushed through the crowd toward them.</p>
<p>He bowed quickly.</p>
<p>“Your Majesty. Archbishop Anselm requests your presence at the cathedral. Riders have arrived from the western parishes.”</p>
<p>“Why?”</p>
<p>The messenger hesitated.</p>
<p>“There is sickness.”</p>
<p>The market seemed to continue around them, but Alaric suddenly noticed how many people had stopped to listen to the bells.</p>
<p>Charles looked toward the cathedral.</p>
<p>“Then the tournament can wait.”</p>
<h1>Chapter 6 – The Chalice of Saint Armand</h1>
<p>By evening, the great hall beside Notre-Dame du Lys no longer looked like a place for ceremonies.</p>
<p>Maps covered the tables.</p>
<p>Letters lay open beneath stones and candlesticks. Priests came and went carrying lists of villages, wells and supplies. Lay brothers counted sacks of grain. Two physicians argued quietly over boiled water. Messengers waited beside the doors with mud still on their boots.</p>
<p>Archbishop Anselm stood at the center of it all.</p>
<p>Alaric had never seen him move so quickly.</p>
<p>“The first reports came five days ago,” Anselm said. “Three villages along the western road. Then seven. Now twelve.”</p>
<p>“What kind of sickness?” Charles asked.</p>
<p>“Fever. Vomiting. Weakness. Mostly among households drawing water from communal wells.”</p>
<p>Alaric looked at the map.</p>
<p>“Then